@* A cell's text kept to exactly one line: the value, optionally followed by the secondary text that would
   otherwise sit under it, clipped with an ellipsis rather than wrapped. Every virtualised row is one line (the
   virtualiser positions rows arithmetically from a single fixed ItemSize), so unbounded free text and a
   MudText stacked under a value are both row-height faults, and this is the shared answer to both.

   Whatever is clipped stays available: the full text (value and secondary together) is the element's title. A
   title rather than a MudTooltip is deliberate, and is the one place JIM does not reach for the component: a
   MudTooltip wraps its child in an inline-flex box of its own, which is precisely the box the ellipsis needs to
   be the block container, so the wrapper would silently defeat the clipping it was added to explain. Where a
   cell's content is markup rather than a string (a linkified description, an icon beside a name), use the
   .jim-one-line class directly instead of this component; the class is the primitive. *@

@if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(Text) && string.IsNullOrEmpty(Secondary))
{
    <EmptyValue />
}
else
{
    <span class="jim-one-line" title="@FullText">
        @Text
        @if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(Secondary))
        {
            <span class="jim-one-line-secondary">@Secondary</span>
        }
    </span>
}

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// The value to show. Null or empty renders the <see cref="EmptyValue"/> placeholder, unless secondary text
    /// is present.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Text { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Text qualifying the value (a setting's description, an attribute's type and plurality, a change's
    /// pattern), rendered low-lighted on the same line rather than as a second block below it.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Secondary { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// What the reader sees on hover, when the value alone is not what should be revealed: a message flattened
    /// for display but held in full here, or a value capped so the hover does not become a wall of text.
    /// Defaults to the value and its secondary text together, since either half can be the part that is clipped.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Tooltip { get; set; }

    private string FullText =>
        Tooltip
        ?? (string.IsNullOrEmpty(Secondary) ? Text ?? string.Empty : $"{Text} ({Secondary})");
}
